.\" UDF filesystem man page
.TH UDF 5 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
udf \- Universal Disk Format filesystem
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B UDF
filesystem is a vendor-neutral filesystem defined by ECMA-167 and the
Optical Storage Technology Association (OSTA). It is commonly used on
optical media (CD, DVD, Blu-ray) and modern USB flash drives.
.PP
UDF supports features including:
.IP \(bu 3
Long filenames (up to 255 Unicode characters)
.IP \(bu
Timestamps with sub-second precision
.IP \(bu
Named streams and extended attributes
.IP \(bu
Large file support (up to 16 EB theoretical)
.IP \(bu
Sparse files
.SS Sector Size
The standard sector size is 2048 bytes for optical media. Other sector
sizes may be used on hard disks and flash media.
.SS Volume Structure
A UDF volume contains:
.TP
.B Anchor Volume Descriptor Pointer (AVDP)
Located at sector 256, points to the Volume Descriptor Sequence.
.TP
.B Volume Descriptor Sequence (VDS)
Contains Primary VD, Partition Descriptor, and Logical Volume Descriptor.
.TP
.B File Set Descriptor (FSD)
Points to the root directory.
.TP
.B File Entry (FE/EFE)
Analogous to Unix inodes, contains file metadata and allocation info.
.TP
.B File Identifier Descriptor (FID)
Directory entries linking names to File Entries.
.SH MOUNT OPTIONS
None currently supported.
.SH LIMITATIONS
This implementation supports UDF 1.50 through 2.60 for read operations and
basic write operations for inline (small) files.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (2),
.BR mkudffs (8)
.SH STANDARDS
ECMA-167, OSTA UDF 2.60
